div.p:
From the evidence of recurring distinctive types in Q2 Lr., Knowles (1982, pp. 195 ff.) identifies a third compositor. He finds that two cases of type were used simultaneously in the last pages of MND and continued in use into sheet I of Lr., and that thereafter only one of these cases continued in use through sheets K and L into the first sheet of H5. Since compositors generally worked each at his own case, two compositors seem to have worked simultaneously at two cases until sheet I(o), whereupon a single compositor finished the job at one of those cases. Doran and Andrews had demonstrated how in many ways this new compositor worked differently from his predecessors; this new study showed additionally that his pattern of spacing commas was quite different from theirs and, incidentally, from that of Folio Compositor B. (The reliability of the spaced-comma test, developed by Howard-Hill, 1973, to distinguish compositors, was challenged by McKenzie, 1984, but vindicated by Jackson, 1987.) Evidently Andrews’s Compositor F in the early quires was really two people, and his Compositor B in the last pages was a third. This pattern of simultaneous work at two cases confirms Blayney’s inference of different compositors setting simultaneously throughout the Pavier quartos; and the new evidence from spacing also confirms Doran’s and Andrews’s conclusions that a new compositor with different habits and preferences took over in quire I of Lr. In fact, as Stone (1980, p. 135 n.) suggests, this new compositor probably began his stint with H(i), since all four of its pages exhibit his markedly heavier punctuation, H1v bears his characteristic spellings Gloster and Glo., and H2 has two Glosters and (as one of his signatures) a proper name in roman type. He seems to have continued at case y through H(i) and I(i) but in the meantime began setting at case x with I(o) and continued at that case in quires K and L. The division of work was as follows:



Compositor 1 (Blayney’s Compositor G), working at case x, set MND F(i), G(i), H(o), Lr. A(o), A(i), C(o), C(i), E(o), F(o), G(o), and H(o);


Compositor 2 (Blayney’s Compositor H), working at case y, set MND F(o), G(o), H(i), Lr. B(o), B(i), D(o), D(i), E(i), F(i), and G(i);


Compositor 3 (Andrews’s Compositor B), working at case y, set H(i) and I(i), and at case x set I(o), K(o), K(i), L(o), L(i), and perhaps part of H5 A.


From the movements of the two skeletons and the sequence of formes, it appears that at sheet G Compositor 1 got somewhat ahead of Compositor 2 and stayed one forme ahead of him for the rest of their collaboration. The probable order of setting was as follows:

A(o) B(o) A(i) B(i) C(o) D(o) C(i) D(i) E(o) F(o) E(i) G(o) F(i) H(o) G(i) H(i) I(o) I(i) K(o) K(i) L(o) L(i).

Wentersdorf (1951, pp. 180–1): Is it really impossible to place Tim near to K L in either 1605 or 1606? The chronology…as so far established, is as follows:



Oth: summer of 1604 (finished before the end of October);

K L: last months of 1605 (after the beginning of October);

Mac: early part of 1606 (possibly after March);

A & C: 1606–07.


It is obvious that there is no room for Tim between K L and Mac, and there are no stylistic affinities which would suggest a date between Mac and A & C. But there is room for it before K L, a whole twelvemonth, if not more. As far as stylistic considerations are concerned, Tim may be placed equally well just before the greater tragedy. This theory is supported by the expert opinion of [Raleigh, 1907, p. 115]: The close likeness between Timon and King Lear has often been noticed, so that it is not unfair to say that in King Lear Shakespeare treated the very theme of Timon, and treated it better.…After King Lear, nothing new could be made of the same figure in a weaker setting. But if, as seems likely, Timon is a first sketch of King Lear, set aside unfinished because the story proved intractable and no full measure of sympathy could be demanded for its hero, the position is explained. I therefore place Tim tentatively in the first half of 1605 [181]…before K L, i.e. in the second half of the 1604–05 season.

sp.p:
Deare father, do not so mistake my words, [B2r]
Nor my playne meaning be misconstrued;
My toung was neuer vsde to flattery.

Nay, if thou talke of reason, then be mute;

For with good reason I can thee confute.[tln 232, 1564–71]

If they, which first by natures sacred law,

Do owe to me the tribute of their liues;

If they to whom I alwayes haue bin kinde,

And bountifull beyond comparison;

If they, for whom I haue vndone my selfe,

And brought my age vnto this extreme want,

Do now reiect, contemne, despise, abhor me,

What reason moueth thee to sorrow for me?

Ah, good my friend, that I should haue thee do,

Is such a thing, as I do shame to speake;

Yet it must needs be done.

Ne’re was I offred such discourtesy;

God and my King, I trust, ere it be long,

Will find a meane to remedy this wrong.

Infayth, I know not how sufficiently;

But the best meane that I can think on, is this:

Ile offer them my dublet in requitall;

For we haue nothing else to spare.

div.p:
Nicoll (1928, pp. 144–55): Out of the older play Shakespeare has retained a number of salient features, which for convenience may be summarised—



(1)the general conduct of the tale to the fourth act, with a marked divergence of the first two scenes;

(2)the headstrong nature of Lear and the frankness and devotion of Cordelia;

(3)the honest figure of Perillus-Kent.

p.quote:
More important are the omissions and the additions in Lear. The parts missed out and deliberately altered may occupy our attention first:



(1)The change from the more historically correct atmosphere of Leir to the mixture of elements in the later Lear. Cornwall is retained, but Cambria becomes Albany. Gaul becomes France and a Duke of Burgundy is inserted. Perillus is similarly changed into the more modern Kent. It may be suggested that these alterations were made for the purpose of rendering the atmosphere of Lear less romantic, of bringing it closer to men’s feelings and experience;

(2)the omission of Leir’s motive in asking for a profession of love from his daughters;

(3)the omission of a motive for Cordelia’s blunt answers;

(4)the crushing of Acts I and II of Leir into a single scene;

(5)the omission of the courting of Cordella by the Gallian King;

(6)the omission of Ragan’s attempt upon Leir’s life [but see 2048];

(7)the omission of human traits in Ragan and Gonorill.

div.p:
Besides these clearly marked omissions and alterations there are a number of additions, of which three are of importance:



(1)The introduction of a sub-plot;

(2)the introduction of the Fool;

(3)the elaboration of the messenger in the figure of Oswald.…


The subplot gives universality; (p. 146) the wise Fool serves as a foil to Lear’s folly, (p. 147) makes the third in a trio of disordered minds, and relieves the audience from the horror of Lear’s madness by making it seem not too evident or too awful.
